==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*nem-}}
From {{af|en|in-|numerable}}; from {{inh|en|enm|innumerable}}, from {{der|en|la|innumerābilis}}, from {{af|la|in-|numerābilis|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪˈnuːməɹ.əbəl/|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɪˈnjuːməɹ.əbəl/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Aquild-innumerable.wav|a=Pacific Northwest}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-innumerable.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Not capable of being [[counted]], [[enumerated]], or [[numbered]].
#: {{ux|en|The casualties of the Second World War were so great that they are '''innumerable'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Connecticut Yankee|passage=Soon we could see the '''innumerable''' banners fluttering, and then the sun struck the sea of armor and set it all aflash.}}
# Of a very high number; extremely numerous.
#* {{quote-book|en
|year=1939
|first=Wilhelm
|last=Filchner
|authorlink=Wilhelm Filchner
|chapter=Issik Pakhta—The Sarts—Good-bye to Borodijin
|translator=E. O. Lorimer
|title=A Scientist in Tartary: from the Hoang-ho to the Indus
|url=https://archive.org/details/dli.pahar.2722/
|location=London
|publisher=w:Faber and Faber Limited
|OCLC=8693414
|page=211
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dli.pahar.2722/page/211/
|text=The wide sandy plain of the valley, some five miles long, which slopes imperceptibly down to Issik Pakhta, is overgrown with minute bushes. All the tiny streams we crossed unite to form the Alessun Gol, which then flows westwards to the pasture grounds of Kala Otlak. We observed dozens of herds of ''orongo'', mainly females, with '''innumerable''' young. The landscape looked highly unpromising for agriculture yet I noticed that the folk of Bulaq Bashi planted grain. Four hours’ marching took us to within sight of Issik Pakhta Nor, which the maps also record as Khon Qum Nor.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1955|month=July|author=M. D. Greville|title=To the Valdres by Rail|journal=Railway Magazine|pages=457-458|text=[[Kjelsås]] (six miles) marks the end of the suburbs, and the line immediately plunges into [[Nordmarka]]. This vast untamed area of forest and mountain (much of it over 2,000 ft. above sea level), interspersed with '''innumerable''' lakes, large and small, is known as [[Oslo]]'s playground.}}

